---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
---

<Layout title="Coverage checklist" description="How SPDTF keeps six semantic outputs, eleven workshop themes, eight formal concerns and four dispositions distinct while checking completeness.">
  <PageMeta category="How we model SPDTF" variant="brand" updated="Coverage framework · not eight separate ontologies" />
  <h1>Coverage checklist</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Coverage is a disciplined way to ask what a candidate may have missed. It combines
    participant-friendly workshop prompts with a formal concern checklist and an explicit
    disposition. It is not a taxonomy of the model and does not require every concern to
    be modelled locally.
  </p>

  <h2 id="four-structures">Four structures, four different jobs</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Do not collapse these structures into one hierarchy</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Structure</th><th scope="col">Count</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Output</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Semantic outputs</th><td>6</td><td>The content practitioners review.</td><td>Glossary, dictionary, taxonomies, vocabularies, resources and relationships.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Workshop themes</th><td>11</td><td>Plain-language prompts for finding omissions.</td><td>Questions and evidence under four lenses.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Formal concerns</th><td>8</td><td>A consistent technical completeness checklist.</td><td>One assessment per concern for each context package.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Dispositions</th><td>4</td><td>A recorded ownership decision for each concern.</td><td><code>model here</code>, <code>reuse shared</code>, <code>boundary contribution</code> or <code>not applicable</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Controlled vocabulary and taxonomy are two workshop prompts but one formal concern.
    Governance and compliance is an operating concern, not a seventh semantic output.
    The common boundary is an architectural agreement, not a ninth ontology concern.
  </p>

  <h2 id="workshop">Four lenses and eleven workshop themes</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Participant-facing completeness prompts</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Lens</th><th scope="col">Themes</th><th scope="col">Questions to ask</th><th scope="col">Formal relationship</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Meaning</th><td>Domain structure; Controlled vocabulary; Taxonomy; Classification</td><td>What things, words, groupings and classifications matter to your work?</td><td>Maps to the first three formal concerns.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Trust</th><td>Governance and compliance; Provenance and quality; Sensitivity and access</td><td>Who supplied the fact, what can it support, and who may use it for which purpose?</td><td>Governance is operating context; the other two map to formal concerns.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Correctness</th><td>Validation constraints; Time and history</td><td>What must be present for this use, and how do state and recorded time change?</td><td>Maps to validation and temporal concerns.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Exchange</th><td>Cross-domain mappings; Common ontology</td><td>What crosses a boundary, and which small meanings must be shared?</td><td>Mapping is formal; the common boundary is architectural.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="formal">Eight formal ontology concerns</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Each concern receives one recorded disposition</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Concern</th><th scope="col">Business question</th><th scope="col">Example evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Domain structure</th><td>What things, identities, relationships and rules matter here?</td><td>Scenarios, definitions, examples and counterexamples.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Vocabulary and taxonomy</th><td>Which terms, values, codes and broader/narrower structures are governed?</td><td>Code lists, glossaries, category trees and stewardship records.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Classification metadata</th><td>Which subject, lifecycle, regulatory and status facets are needed?</td><td>Search, reporting and operational classification needs.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Provenance and quality</th><td>What source, attribution, derivation, confidence and quality evidence is required?</td><td>Source registers, lineage, quality measures and authority statements.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Access control and data sensitivity</th><td>Which sensitivity, purpose, consent, role and authorisation semantics apply?</td><td>Policy and participant scenarios; never runtime access rules alone.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Validation and constraints</th><td>Which completeness, consistency and business expectations can be tested?</td><td>Positive and negative examples with agreed rules.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Temporal state and history</th><td>How are valid time, recorded time, states and version chains represented?</td><td>Lifecycle examples and correction histories.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Cross-domain mappings</th><td>Which meaning-preserving translations are needed at boundaries?</td><td>Paired definitions, examples and qualified comparison evidence.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Cross-domain mappings are Category 8 in the adopted completeness framework: a
    cross-cutting bridge concern, not a ninth content ontology. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">mapping method</a>
    explains the human decision gate, SKOS predicates and why SSSOM is not currently
    selected for internal mapping records.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dispositions">Four allowed dispositions</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Disposition guide for each formal concern</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Disposition</th><th scope="col">Use when</th><th scope="col">Required record</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>model here</code></th><td>The context owns substantive domain meaning.</td><td>Owner, evidence, candidate constructs and review questions.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>reuse shared</code></th><td>A common or externally governed profile already meets the need.</td><td>Exact version, terms, mechanism and conformance boundary.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>boundary contribution</code></th><td>The context supplies requirements or mappings to Interoperability.</td><td>Consumer need, affected contexts and proposed agreement.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>not applicable</code></th><td>The concern genuinely does not apply to this scope.</td><td>A reviewable rationale and re-open trigger.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="apply">How to apply the checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Run the eleven themes as a facilitated conversation; capture unanswered questions.</li>
    <li>Assess each of the eight concerns against attributed evidence and competency questions.</li>
    <li>Choose exactly one disposition per concern and name the accountable owner.</li>
    <li>Link any modelled concern to the relevant semantic outputs and technical artefacts.</li>
    <li>Record gaps, dissent, dependencies and re-open triggers instead of filling them with assumptions.</li>
    <li>Repeat the assessment when evidence, scope, standards profile or candidate version changes.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Completeness does not mean “model everything.” A well-justified reuse or
    not-applicable decision is better than duplicating shared terms or inventing content
    for a concern that has no evidence.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/standards">Next: inspect the standards profile →</a></p>
</Layout>
